webrtc-audio-processing (standalone WebRTC Audio Processing Module, AEC3)

Source:   https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/pulseaudio/webrtc-audio-processing.git
Tag:      v2.1
Commit:   846fe90a289f58b7c9303a635142aa2c7caa93e5
Subproject: abseil-cpp 20240722.0 (meson wrap, statically bundled into the archive)

Build (macOS arm64, static, release):
  export MACOSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET=15.6
  meson setup build -Ddefault_library=static --buildtype=release
  ninja -C build
  meson install -C build --destdir=<staging>

Vendored artifacts:
  ../WebRTCAPM.xcframework  — the static archive plus both header roots, which
                              is what Packages/Audio declares as a binaryTarget
  licenses/                 — upstream BSD-3 (WebRTC) and Apache-2.0 (abseil)

The archive is self-contained (APM + rtc_base + common_audio + abseil). The
staging tree the build above installs has this shape:

  lib/libwebrtc-audio-processing-2.a
  include/webrtc-audio-processing-2/   public headers (installed set)
  include/absl/                       abseil headers the public headers include

Only the xcframework is checked in, so the repository carries one copy of the
archive and one copy of the headers rather than two.

Regenerating the xcframework from a fresh build of the staging tree above,
from Packages/Audio/Vendor/:

  xcodebuild -create-xcframework \
    -library <staging>/lib/libwebrtc-audio-processing-2.a \
    -headers <staging>/include \
    -output WebRTCAPM.xcframework

`-headers` takes the include root, NOT include/webrtc-audio-processing-2:
-create-xcframework accepts only one headers directory per library, and both
roots are needed on the search path, so the parent is packaged and the target
puts both it and its webrtc-audio-processing-2 subdirectory on the path (see
the header search paths in Packages/Audio/Package.swift).

Consumer cflags (per upstream pkg-config): -DWEBRTC_POSIX

The xcframework carries a single macos-arm64 slice, matching Echo's
Apple-Silicon-only platform floor. Adding a slice means changing the
Package's header search paths, which name that slice directly.
